{
  "teamId": 113,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Cincinnati Reds entered the season with genuine optimism, buoyed by a power-laden roster and a revamped rotation headlined by newly acquired Sonny Gray. But April quickly humbled them. Raisel Iglesias, normally one of the most reliable closers in the National League, was ambushed repeatedly in high-leverage moments — a Ryan Braun double on Opening Day, then a Kevin Newman double days later — setting an early tone of near-misses and frustration. A brutal 11-16 April left the Reds buried in the standings before May had even arrived.\n\nMay and June offered a genuine reprieve. The offense found its footing, led by slugger Eugenio Suárez, whose home run barrage was becoming one of the best power seasons in franchise history. A clutch grand slam in late May swung a pivotal game, and the Reds rattled off a 15-13 May to claw back toward respectability. In June, younger players like Nick Senzel began flashing the promise that made fans believe the rebuild was real, and a late-inning comeback against Houston featuring Senzel and Jesse Winker gave the city a brief taste of what this team could be. At 11-13 in June, they were still fighting.\n\nJuly and August told the story of a team that could not hold a lead. Iglesias surrendered a devastating grand slam to Tyler Saladino in late July, while Robert Stephenson and Michael Lorenzen were torched for multi-run blasts that erased late-inning cushions. Ronald Acuña carved up Cincinnati relievers on back-to-back nights in early August. The Reds went 12-13 in July and 13-16 in August, the bullpen functioning as a revolving door of heartbreak. Individual heroes — Tucker Barnhart's three-run blast, Eugenio Suárez answering Saladino's slam with one of his own — kept games interesting, but the wins were too few.\n\nSeptember was a fitting coda to a 75-87 season full of tantalizing moments and structural flaws. Fringe contributors like Phillip Ervin, Brian O'Grady, and Freddy Galvis provided some late-season fireworks — Galvis's grand slam on September 12th was a highlight in an otherwise fading campaign — but Raisel Iglesias saved his worst for last, allowing a Kevin Newman walk-off home run on September 27th that encapsulated the season's cruelest pattern. The Reds finished twelve games under .500, an organization still searching for the formula to turn raw power and flashes of brilliance into sustained October relevance.",
